<h2 id="hardiness">Hardiness zones</h2>
<p>Each zone from 1a to 13b has its own page, with the temperature range and every crop in this catalogue whose <em>sourced</em> range includes that half-zone: see <a href="{{ '/zones/' | url }}">Hardiness zones</a>. Crops with no sourced range are not listed on any zone page. A range given as a whole zone covers both halves.</p>
<p>A plant hardiness zone (USDA-style) describes how cold winters get: it is based on the <strong>average yearly extreme minimum temperature</strong>.
Zone 1 starts at −51.1 °C (−60 °F); each zone is 10 °F (about 5.6 °C) wide and split into a colder <em>a</em> and a warmer <em>b</em> half (for example 8b = −9.4 to −6.7 °C).</p>
<p><strong>Your zone</strong> is worked out in your browser: OpenFarm asks the <a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-weather-api">Open-Meteo historical weather API</a> (ERA5 reanalysis, about 25 km grid, CC BY 4.0) for the daily minimum temperatures of 1991–2020 at your location, takes the coldest night of each year and averages them.
The result is cached in this browser. Limits: a 25 km grid smooths out frost pockets, valleys and city heat, and reanalysis tends to be a little milder than station records in cold spots – treat the zone as a guide, not a guarantee.</p>
<p><strong>Plant ranges</strong> (e.g. “zones 5–9”) come from cited sources – currently the <a href="https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/">NC State Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox</a>. Crops without a sourced range show none; we never estimate one. When your zone is outside a plant's range, its page shows a warning.</p>

<h2 id="calendar">The planting calendar</h2>
<p>A coloured month is a sourced window from that guide: sow indoors, sow outside, plant out, prune, or harvest. Hover or tap the month for a short note. When the guide already records them, the note adds sun and water for sowing outside, spacing for planting out, days to maturity for harvest, and up to two tools listed for that kind of job. It does not add advice the guide does not have.</p>
<p>On a city page one month can hold more than one job, and the note names those jobs. A southern-hemisphere city shifts a northern guide by six months and says that the shift is unverified.</p>

<h2 id="countries">Countries</h2>
<p>Each country and territory has a page at <a href="{{ '/countries/' | url }}">/countries/</a> (for example <a href="{{ '/countries/nl/' | url }}">/countries/nl/</a>) with a climate map (Leaflet, with a static SVG fallback), approximate climate-region coverage, a data-only “Grows well here” list of crops that have a sourced guide for those regions, major cities, and seed sources listed for that country. Climate shares are approximate area-weighted fractions of the 0.5° Beck et al. (2018) Köppen grid (CC BY 4.0) assigned with Natural Earth 50m boundaries (public domain). Map outlines are simplified from those Natural Earth boundaries (public domain). Tiny territories use a GeoNames city sample instead of an area share; the method is spelled out on the country page. Names and cities are from GeoNames (CC BY 4.0). Region pages list the countries they cover.</p>
<p>Selected cities have their own page at <a href="{{ '/places/' | url }}">/places/&lt;slug&gt;/</a>, using the same slugs as <code>?place=</code> links (for example <a href="{{ '/places/amsterdam-nl/' | url }}">/places/amsterdam-nl/</a>). A city page has a map pin, the Köppen climate region at that point, a hardiness zone derived from Open-Meteo ERA5 1991–2020 (labelled as derived, not an official USDA map), a country link, the same data-only “Grows well here” rule, and a sowing and harvest calendar from sourced regional guides. Standing pages cover each territory’s capital (when it is in GeoNames cities15000) and the up to 12 most populous cities already listed on the country page; other cities still work with <code>?place=</code> and search.</p>

<h2 id="unreviewed">The “Unreviewed” badge</h2>
<p>Some crop and variety pages come from the original OpenFarm's community data and have not been checked against sources yet. They carry a small <span class="badge unrev">Unreviewed</span> badge. Facts on these pages may be wrong – don't rely on them for edibility or safety, and don't treat wiki-derived spacing or companions as authoritative. Each one has an <em>Improve this page</em> link; once someone checks it against sources it loses the badge.</p>
<p>Some variety pages are name-only records from Wikidata (CC0): the cultivar name and, when Wikidata has it, the taxon string. They use the same badge, and they do not include growing instructions. They are not a source for spacing, sun, sowing or companions.</p>


<h2 id="profile">Your profile and exports</h2>
<p>Your location, favourites (the heart button on a crop, variety, guide or tool), list filters and “what I'm growing” list are <strong>stored only in this browser</strong> (localStorage) – nothing is sent to a server, and there is no account. Changes save automatically (notes after a short pause) and a brief “Saved” notice confirms it. Open tabs stay in sync. Clearing your browser data removes it. On <a href="{{ '/profile/' | url }}">Profile</a> you can export it:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>JSON</strong> – a full backup that you can import again (on another browser or device). Imports are validated before anything is replaced.</li>
  <li><strong>CSV</strong> – your favourites and growing list with status, sow/plant dates, bed, quantity and notes, for spreadsheets.</li>
  <li><strong>Calendar (.ics)</strong> – yearly sow, plant and harvest reminders for the crops you grow, from your region's guides; works in Google Calendar, Apple Calendar and Outlook.</li>
  <li><strong>Markdown</strong> – a readable garden notebook.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can share a location with a readable link such as <a href="{{ '/' | url }}?place=amsterdam-nl"><code>?place=amsterdam-nl</code></a>, and the same slug is used for city pages such as <a href="{{ '/places/amsterdam-nl/' | url }}"><code>/places/amsterdam-nl/</code></a>.
  If two cities in the same country share a name, the link includes a region code, for example <code>?place=portland-or-us</code> or <code>/places/portland-or-us/</code> when that city has a standing page.
  Older <code>?lat=…&amp;lon=…</code> links still work. A map click that is not near a city in the offline list keeps the coordinates in the URL.</p>
<p>“Clear my data” removes the profile and reminder state from this browser.</p>

<h2 id="notifications">Reminders and notifications</h2>
<p>The reminders bell shows reminders worked out in your browser from your growing list, your region's calendar and the weather forecast (frost, dry or very wet days). Browser notifications are optional and only asked for when you click the button. Because there is no server, reminders only appear while a page of the site is open – use the <strong>.ics export</strong> to get them in your calendar when the site is closed.</p>

<h2 id="labels">Filters and labels</h2>
<p>Crop cards show the plant photo and names (a coloured left edge marks the category on mixed lists). The Unreviewed note appears on the crop or variety page, not on the card. Open <em>Filters &amp; labels</em> above a list to show extra labels (number of guides and varieties, annual/perennial, hardiness zones, Köppen codes) or to show only crops with a guide, reviewed crops or perennials. Your choice is remembered in this browser.</p>

<h2 id="sources">Sources &amp; licences</h2>
<p>OpenFarm content is <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, attributed to “OpenFarm contributors”.
  Imported records that already say CC0 stay CC0. The site’s code is MIT.</p>
<p><strong>Imported data</strong> (values copied or adapted into our files) may only come from a source whose own terms are public domain or CC0, CC BY, or CC BY-SA.
  NC, ND, all-rights-reserved, unclear terms, and copyleft code (GPL, AGPL) are not imported. Each source in
  <code>content/sources.yaml</code> has a <code>license</code> and a <code>use</code> of either <code>import</code> or <code>citation</code>.
  The build fails if an import source is not on that allowlist.</p>
<p><strong>Citations</strong> are different. Extension services, the RHS and similar pages are usually all-rights-reserved.
  We summarise their advice in our own words and link to them; we do not paste their text. Citing a work does not change our licence.</p>
<p><strong>Wikidata</strong> (CC0) supplies scientific names, families, and some cultivar names. Those cultivar pages record the name only. They do not include growing instructions. OpenPlantDB’s bulk file was reviewed on 12 November 2026 and not imported: every source URL in it points at a seed shop or nursery, and the other rows give no link for the number or sentence. Its unmerged generated draft was not imported either. The Wind River variety compilation was also left out, because it is built from seed-catalogue feeds. The allowlist is in <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/SOURCES.md">SOURCES.md</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Wiki-derived data is not an authority.</strong> Many crop pages started from the CC0 OpenFarm.cc rescue dataset.
  That dataset’s own README warns it is a scaffold. Spacing, sun, sowing method, prose and companions from that import stay
  <a href="#unreviewed">Unreviewed</a> until someone checks them against a published source. Do not add new companion or spacing
  claims from wiki-derived sets.</p>
<p><strong>Edibility</strong> (<code>edible</code> / <code>edible_parts</code>) is filled only from an authoritative, compatible source and is cited on the record.
  If we have no such source, the fields stay blank. Sourced toxic parts are listed when we have them.
  Edible-part info is for cultivated crops. Don't use it to identify wild plants or for foraging.</p>
<p><strong>Contributor credits</strong> on a page come from that file’s git history (GitHub usernames, no emails, bots omitted).
  The Sources list is published references only. A one-time credit links the rescue import to the original OpenFarm.cc dataset.</p>
<p>The full policy, with licence links and exclusions (including Growstuff’s AGPL code versus its CC BY-SA structured data), is in
  <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/SOURCES.md">SOURCES.md</a>.</p>

<h2 id="api">JSON API</h2>
<p>All data is available as static JSON (no key needed). There is a longer reference, with example requests and reuse rules, at
  <a href="{{ '/api/' | url }}">/api/</a>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><a href="{{ '/api/all.json' | url }}"><code>/api/all.json</code></a> – everything (regions, crops with varieties and guides, problems, tools, seed sources, companions)</li>
  <li><a href="{{ '/api/guides.json' | url }}"><code>/api/guides.json</code></a> – compact guide index</li>
  <li><a href="{{ '/api/tools.json' | url }}"><code>/api/tools.json</code></a> – tools</li>
  <li><a href="{{ '/api/countries.json' | url }}"><code>/api/countries.json</code></a> – countries</li>
  <li><code>/crops/&lt;crop&gt;.json</code> (e.g. <a href="{{ '/crops/tomato.json' | url }}">tomato</a>), <code>/regions/&lt;region&gt;.json</code>, <code>/countries/&lt;iso2&gt;.json</code>, <code>/places/&lt;slug&gt;.json</code>, and <code>/zones/&lt;zone&gt;.json</code></li>
</ul>
<p>Guide URLs in the JSON are now <code>/crops/&lt;crop&gt;/&lt;region&gt;/</code> (old <code>/guides/…</code> HTML pages redirect).
  Content is CC BY-SA 4.0 (imported records CC0); credit “OpenFarm contributors” and keep ShareAlike for adaptations.</p>

<h2 id="contribute">Contributing</h2>
<p>Everything is plain Markdown in the <a href="{{ site.repo }}">GitHub repository</a>. Each page has one button at the bottom – <em>Edit this guide / crop / variety</em> – that opens the file on GitHub. Guides need real sources (extension services, national horticultural societies, FAO and similar). See <a href="{{ '/contribute/' | url }}">About &amp; contribute</a> and <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">CONTRIBUTING.md</a> for the details.</p>
